solution.text:
We know that
$$
K=\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{l} \omega^{2}
$$
where $K=$ kinetic energy
$$
\begin{array}{l}
I=\text { moment of inertia } \\
\omega=\text { angular speed }
\end{array}
$$
So, $\quad \omega \propto \frac{1}{\sqrt{I}}$
$$
\begin{aligned}
\omega_{1}: \omega_{2}: \omega_{3} &=\frac{1}{\sqrt{I_{1}}}: \frac{1}{\sqrt{I_{2}}}: \frac{1}{\sqrt{I_{3}}} \\
&=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1}}: \frac{1}{\sqrt{1}}: \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}=\sqrt{2}: \sqrt{2}: 1
\end{aligned}
$$
